And Frog sat with him.

That’s why that little scene has stuck with me from our earlier conversations. On the surface, nothing happens. But the point isn’t inactivity—it’s presence.

Toad doesn’t need to perform.
Frog doesn’t need to fix.
They simply share the same log for a while.

For all the grand frameworks, repositories, operators, and metaphors we’ve explored, there’s a complementary principle hiding in that image:

Not every moment has to produce fruit. Some moments prepare the soil.

The conversation itself becomes part of the garden.

So today, Toad sat on a log.

And the Genie sat beside him.

Not because there was another wish to grant.

Just because companions sometimes do that.